#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161799 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7076c0-fa73-5cb1-9206-2b129dd08be1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "79fbc4ff-042e-5454-9fa4-f2582c10a51a" :question/math-academy-id "e-9132" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -9\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=-1$, and $\\lambda_3=3$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix with the following eigenvalues:\n\n- $\\lambda=-1$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$\n- $\\lambda=3$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$\n\nAs a result, we have one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block $J_1(3)$ corresponding to $\\lambda=3$.\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to\n\n$$\n\\lambda=-1{:}\n$$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block $J_2(-1)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chain:\n$\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0}$\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1 \\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely $J_1(-1) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(-1)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chains:\n$v_{1}→^(A + I)0; v_{2}→^(A + I)0$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda=-1$.\n\nComputing $A+I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + I &= \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 7 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -9\\end{bmatrix} + \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA + I &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-1)R_{1} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -8 & -8\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{3}:=R_{3} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 8 & 8 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A+I)=2\n$$\n\nthere will be two eigenvectors that correspond to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$, say, $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$. So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-1$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0} \\[5pt] \\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A+I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case II*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161800 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "83ce5e23-e0ef-5f59-afe8-5c63d901b290" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Only One Eigenvalue"}] :question/id #uuid "b281dc8a-0cef-50c5-96f4-b61dbcea27f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-9133" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=\\lambda_2=\\lambda_3=2$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a non-scalar $3 \\times 3$ matrix that has the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$ of algebraic multiplicity $3$.\n\nAs a result, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to\n\n$$\n\\lambda=2{:}\n$$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $3 \\times 3$ Jordan block $J_3(2)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ corresponding to $\\lambda=2$ form the following chain:\n$\\mathbf{v}_3 \\xrightarrow{A-2I} \\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-2I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-2I} \\mathbf{0}$\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of one $2 \\times 2$ and one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block, say $J_2(2) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(2)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1, \\mathbf v_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ corresponding to $\\lambda=2$ form the following chains:\n$v_{2}→^(A - 2 I)v_{1}→^(A - 2 I)0; v_{3}→^(A - 2 I)0$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda=2$.\n\nComputing $A-2I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 2I &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix} - 2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 2I &= \\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}↔R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1 & 3 \\\\ -1 & 1 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + (-2)R_{1} \\\\\n&= R_{3}:=R_{3} + R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-2I)=1\n$$\n\nthere will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$, say, $\\mathbf{v}_1$. So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=2$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_3 \\xrightarrow{A-2 I} \\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-2 I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-2 I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\n**Note:** The Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161801 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d60456d7-669b-5e35-84bf-81b594b18f3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Three Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/id #uuid "1151303f-2da6-5587-beda-69ff592a5ca3" :question/math-academy-id "e-9134" :question/problem "$\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 8 & 8 & 8 \\\\ -4 & -2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=8$, $\\lambda_2=7$, and $\\lambda_3=4$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix." :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given a $3 \\times 3$ matrix that has precisely $3$ distinct eigenvalues.\n\nTherefore, $A$ is diagonalizable, and its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nA Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks. So, another Jordan canonical form of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 8\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d60456d7-669b-5e35-84bf-81b594b18f3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Three Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b35a216b-645a-5866-9bad-8c82b805a8c3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158839} {:answer/id #uuid "b98f01f7-c21e-5756-9fde-8a6537a40dec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158840} {:answer/id #uuid "02f31b4c-ec80-5be4-96c9-cb0cb51a9c04" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158841} {:answer/id #uuid "3679e66f-a526-5f6b-b617-ad2da7319af4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158842} {:answer/id #uuid "5e3a13d5-dc56-5c06-8677-153e8913590d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5e3a13d5-dc56-5c06-8677-153e8913590d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158843} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a9db42-24b2-55a7-899b-dfed4b330dd9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158838}] :question/id #uuid "132107ab-2199-5d46-b51c-37977a6efa1d" :question/math-academy-id "q-109390" :question/problem "$A = \\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 0 & -4 \\\\ -1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nThe matrix above has eigenvalues $λ_{1} = 2$, $λ_{2} = 1$, and $λ_{3} =-3$. Find a Jordan canonical form of the matrix."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162218 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5a7076c0-fa73-5cb1-9206-2b129dd08be1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Jordan Canonical Form of a 3x3 Matrix With Two Distinct Eigenvalues"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cbc64cd6-c4c1-5218-bab6-01676d7df07b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158851} {:answer/id #uuid "e599ef31-9f85-5982-98ff-b7aeaf59e29a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 